WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.132 --> 00:00:02.090
SANDEEP DINESH: Everyone
knows it's a good idea

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:05.090
to keep your application
dependencies up to date.

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:08.420
Kubernetes and Docker can make
these updates much easier,

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:11.060
as you can build a new container
with the updated dependencies

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:13.730
and deploy it with
relative ease.

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:15.890
Just like your
application dependencies,

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:18.800
Kubernetes is constantly getting
new features and security

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:19.860
updates.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.730
So the underlying nodes and
Kubernetes' infrastructure

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:25.220
needs to be kept
up to date as well.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:27.371
In this episode of
"Kubernetes Best Practices,"

00:00:27.371 --> 00:00:29.120
let's take a look at
how Google Kubernetes

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:32.119
Engine can make upgrading your
Kubernetes cluster painless.

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:35.451
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:42.080
When it comes to
upgrading your cluster,

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:44.360
there are two parts that
need to be upgraded--

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.150
the masters and the nodes.

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:49.640
The masters need to
be updated first,

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:51.470
and then the nodes can follow.

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:54.980
Let's see how to upgrade both
using Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:57.350
Google Kubernetes Engine
will automatically

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:00.660
upgrade the master as point
releases are released.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.830
However, it usually won't
upgrade to a new version--

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:07.550
for example, 1.7 to
1.8 automatically.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:09.780
When you're ready to
upgrade to a new version,

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.360
you can just click
the Upgrade Master

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:14.150
button in the GKE console.

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:16.430
However, you may have
noticed that the dialog

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:17.960
box has the following.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:21.140
Changing the master version
can result in several minutes

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:23.120
of control plane downtime.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.350
During that period, you'll be
unable to edit this cluster.

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:27.440
Wow, OK.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.810
So when the master goes
down for the upgrade,

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:32.510
deployments, and services, and
the all that normal Kubernetes

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:35.700
stuff will continue
to work as expected.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:39.020
However, anything that
requires the Kubernetes API

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:40.250
will stop working.

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:42.590
So this means [? QPTL ?]
will sop working,

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:44.660
applications that use
the Kubernetes API to get

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:47.390
information about the
cluster will stop working,

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:49.880
and basically, you can't make
any changes to the cluster

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:52.300
while it's being upgraded.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:56.020
So how do you update the
master with zero downtime?

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:58.720
While the standard zonal
GKE cluster can only

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:00.790
have one master
backing them, you

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:03.220
can actually create
regional clusters

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:07.090
that provide multi-zone
high-availability masters.

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:08.650
So when creating
your cluster, be

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:11.080
sure to select the
regional option.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:12.190
And that's it.

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:14.590
Your nodes and masters
will automatically

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:17.860
be created in three
zones, and the masters

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:21.170
will be behind a
load-balanced IP address.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:22.930
So the Kubernetes
API will continue

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:25.420
to work during an upgrade.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:28.390
When upgrading nodes, there
are a few different strategies

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:29.550
that you can use.

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:31.600
There's two that I
want to focus on--

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:36.010
rolling updates, and
migration using node pools.

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:38.920
The simplest way to update
your Kubernetes nodes

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:40.960
is using a rolling update.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:42.760
This is the default
upgrade mechanism

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:46.170
that GKE uses to
update your nodes.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.820
A rolling update works
in the following way.

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:51.760
One by one, a node is
drained and cordoned

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:55.210
so that there are no more
pods running on that node.

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:57.110
Then the node is
deleted, and the new node

00:02:57.110 --> 00:03:00.090
is created with the
updated Kubernetes version.

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:02.000
Once that node is
up and running,

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.390
the next node is updated,
and so on and so forth,

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:06.820
until all the nodes are updated.

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:08.920
You can let GKE manage
this process for you

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:14.650
completely by enabling automatic
node upgrades on the node pool.

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:16.210
Now, if you don't
select this option,

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:20.610
the GKE dashboard will alert you
when an upgrade is available.

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:22.750
Just click the link,
follow the prompts,

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:25.180
and you'll begin
your rolling update.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:27.910
It's important to make
sure your pods are managed

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:30.940
by a replica set, deployment,
stateful set, or something

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:31.840
similar.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:34.790
Standalone pods
won't be rescheduled.

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:38.320
So while a rolling update
is simple to perform on GKE,

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:40.460
it does have a few drawbacks.

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:42.760
One drawback is you
actually get one less node

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:44.640
of capacity in your cluster.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:46.360
While it's updating,
that node is

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:48.460
being taken out of commission.

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:52.300
But this issue is easily solved
by scaling up your node pool

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:54.550
to add extra capacity,
and then scaling it

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:57.130
back down once the
upgrade is finished.

00:03:57.130 --> 00:04:00.130
Also, the fully automated
nature of the rolling update

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:02.950
makes it easy to do, but
you have less control

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:04.600
over the process.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:07.810
So if something goes wrong
and you had to rollback,

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:09.739
it takes time to roll
back to the old version,

00:04:09.739 --> 00:04:11.530
because you have to
stop the rolling update

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:13.960
and then undo it.

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:16.180
So now let's look at how
you can use multiple node

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:18.519
pools to upgrade your cluster.

00:04:18.519 --> 00:04:21.160
So instead of upgrading
the active node pool,

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:23.200
as you would with
the rolling update,

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:26.260
you create a brand
new fresh node pool,

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:28.240
wait for all the
nodes to be running,

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:32.270
and then migrate workloads
over one node at a time.

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:34.270
Because you're running
these commands yourself,

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:37.310
you have more control over
the migration process.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:41.380
But GKE is still managing
the nodes for you.

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:43.630
So let's assume that our
Kubernetes cluster has

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:45.940
three VMs right now.

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:50.150
You can see the nodes
with the get node command.

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:52.370
To create a new node
pool with the name

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:55.920
pool two, run the
following command.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:57.940
Remember to customize
this command

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:01.050
so the new node pool is
the same as the old pool.

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:03.285
You can also use the
GUI to create a new node

00:05:03.285 --> 00:05:04.350
pool if you want.

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:07.489
Check out the link
for more information.

00:05:07.489 --> 00:05:09.030
So now if you check
the nodes, you'll

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:13.140
notice that there's three more
nodes with the new pool name.

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:17.010
However, the pods will
still be on the old nodes.

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:18.810
So let's move them over.

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:22.080
We need to move pods
to the new node pool.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:25.930
Let's move over one node at
a time in a rolling fashion.

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:29.120
First, cordon each
of the old nodes.

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:32.260
This will prevent new pods
from being scheduled onto them.

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:34.870
Once all the old
nodes are cordoned,

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:37.990
pods can only be scheduled
onto the new nodes.

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:41.080
This means you can start to
remove pods from the old nodes,

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:43.180
and Kubernetes will
automatically schedule them

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:45.440
onto the new nodes.

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:47.510
Then we need to drain each node.

00:05:47.510 --> 00:05:50.290
So this will delete all
the pods on that node.

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:53.290
After you drain a node,
make sure the new pods

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:56.390
are up and running before
moving on to the next one.

00:05:56.390 --> 00:05:58.650
If you have any issues
during migration,

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:02.554
uncordon the old pool, and then
cordon and drain the new pool.

00:06:02.554 --> 00:06:03.970
And so the pods
will automatically

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:06.950
get rescheduled back
to the old pool.

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:09.440
Once all the pods are
safely rescheduled,

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:12.380
it's just time to
delete the old pool.

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:16.190
Replace default-pool with
the pool you want to delete.

00:06:16.190 --> 00:06:18.200
By using Google
Kubernetes Engine,

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:20.000
you can keep your
Kubernetes cluster up

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:22.400
to date with just a few clicks.

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:25.520
I highly recommend using
GKE regional clusters

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:27.830
for the high-availability
masters,

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:29.720
and using automatic
node upgrades

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:33.050
to have a hassle-free
upgrade experience.

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:35.780
If you need extra control
for your node updates,

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:37.670
using node pools
gives you that control

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:41.180
without giving up the advantages
of the manage platform

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:43.580
that GKE gives you.

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:46.220
If you're not using a
manage service like GKE,

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:49.310
you can still use a rolling
update or node pools method

00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:51.710
with your own cluster
to upgrade nodes.

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:54.620
The difference is, you need
to manually add the new nodes

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:56.930
to your cluster and
perform the master upgrades

00:06:56.930 --> 00:06:59.450
yourself, which can be tricky.

00:06:59.450 --> 00:07:02.560
I'll see you on the next episode
of "Kubernetes Best Practices."

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:05.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

